ed truck tier draw, a repetition penalty, difficulty-weighted tire categories, and the smallest supported tire size/first variant. Regional variety counts saved assignments, pending edits, and new selections together. HEAVY_DUTY selections favor trucks within one target tier, widening by one tier when that offers less-used compatible models, then choose among the least-used regional models before applying progression weights. This prevents a lone low-tier candidate from being selected repeatedly while suitable alternatives exist. Other classes receive an additional strong regional repeat penalty. Necessary reuse and tier-band widening are explained in detailed logs. If the compatible nearby pool has fewer than three trucks or all its trucks have been used, there is an 18% chance of a similarly ranked OFFROAD substitute, capped at one per five saved HEAVY_DUTY assignments (the first opportunity is assignment five). The exception is one-way, still requires ownership and addon/tire compatibility, and is reported only in detailed logs. The cap includes persisted assignments across runs and ownership changes; existing assignments stay fixed. Required listed addons are preserved through compatible equivalents (including sideboard capacity); incompatible trucks are rejected and another candidate is tried. Unranked regions/trucks, unverified placements, missions, and unavailable configurations are not guessed. Saved assignments remain fixed across restarts and ownership changes. This pane shows region completion/error messages without revealing choices. Show detailed logs is off by default; enabling it reveals recorded placement messages in their original order, including tire-category variation, compatibility fallbacks, and rejected candidates, without naming replacement trucks. Actual trucks and equipment are shown in Game data → Regions-Trucks. That tab has Reset truck to original and Reset selected regions to original buttons, which clear assignments and queue restoration of the original model and exact map equipment. You can rerandomize a reset preview immediately, without applying the restoration first. Start → Apply map changes performs all map writes, carrying the chosen tires/addons through preview and Apply. Start also hides randomized replacement details so applying does not spoil the result. Old manual pending-change arrays are read and upgraded atomically when saved. Mission randomization is not configured. Editable rule text remains descriptive notes; editing prose does not change the implemented constraints.
 
 A simple Windows Forms frontend is in `src/SnowPakTool.Desktop`. Its first tab handles PAK unpacking, workspace merging, and repacking. The **Experience / Ranks** tab displays and edits the `rankExp` progression values in `[ps]/rank_settings.sso`, shows the 17-season maximum of 1,163,997 XP, provides a collapsible region XP checklist with a selected subtotal, and totals the current rank progression values needed to reach level 30. The updated XP progression uses an exponential curve, with a steepness slider beside the curve preview and an XP reduction slider below it; the reduction amount appears beneath that slider. Opening the tab, restoring control settings, or adjusting the preview does not stage any file changes. Save rank XP saves an explicit snapshot in per-workspace app preferences. Start → Apply pending PAK edits writes only that snapshot to the workspace, preserving the original; later preview edits are excluded until saved again. Staged XP survives restarts and is listed on Start even before the rank tab is opened. Apply staged rank XP before packing.
 
